{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-tangent-lines.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Higher derivatives and tangent lines",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Take second derivatives, find the slope of a curve at a point, and draw the tangent line there.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let f = x^3 - 3x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(f, x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`diff(f, x, 2)` differentiates twice: the second derivative, which measures how the slope itself changes.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(f, x, 2)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Slope at a point",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The slope at one point is the derivative with a number put in for $x$: `subst(e, x, v)` substitutes.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst(diff(f, x), x, 2)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The tangent line",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Tangent line\n> The tangent line to $y = f(x)$ at $x = a$ is $y = f(a) + f'(a)\\,(x - a)$: the line through the point with the curve's slope there.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let a = 2",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "slider": {
        "min": -2,
        "max": 2,
        "step": 1
      },
      "mode": "raw"
    },
    {
      "src": "let s = subst(diff(f, x), x, a)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let t = subst(f, x, a) + s*(x - a)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([f, t], x, -2.5, 2.5)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Drag `a`. At which points is the tangent flat? There $f'(a) = 3a^2 - 3 = 0$, so $a = \\pm 1$: the top of the hump and the bottom of the dip.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst(diff(x^2, x), x, 3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "What is the slope of $y = x^2$ at $x = 3$?",
      "hints": [
        "The slope is the derivative, $2x$, at $x = 3$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(x^4, x, 2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Find the second derivative of $x^4$.",
      "hints": [
        "Differentiate twice: $x^4 \\to 4x^3 \\to \\dots$"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "subst(diff(f, x, 2), x, 1)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "For the $f$ above, what is $f''(1)$?",
      "hints": [
        "$f''(x) = 6x$ (the cell above shows it)."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> The derivative at a point is a number, the slope there; the tangent line is the straight line with that slope through the point. Where the slope is zero the curve turns.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
